WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.960
Tension on the set of a Christmas movie? Who&nbsp;
butted heads to keep an "Elf" sequel from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.840
happening? The answer is actually pretty&nbsp;
surprising. "Elf 2" will never happen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.600
Keep watching to find out why.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.920
Comedy legend Will Ferrell's career has had its&nbsp;
shares of ups and downs, but when it comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:20.880
Christmas comedy "Elf," the actor delivered&nbsp;
some serious holiday cheer. Directed by Jon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:26.960
Favreau and written by David Berenbaum, the&nbsp;
2003 film quickly became a hit. Both critics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:32.000
and audiences applauded the film for its blend&nbsp;
of endearing goofball humor, which is evidenced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:39.760
by its 85% critics score and 79% audience score on&nbsp;
Rotten Tomatoes. The movie stars Ferrell as Buddy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:45.520
a human accidentally transported to the North Pole&nbsp;
as a baby, who now works as one of Santa's helpers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:50.880
among his army of elves. After discovering his&nbsp;
true identity one Christmas, Buddy heads to New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:56.240
York City to meet his biological father. The&nbsp;
result is equal parts funny and heartwarming.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:01.920
Now considered a Christmas classic, the cast&nbsp;
includes Hollywood icons Ed Asner and Bob Newhart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.000
who star as Santa and Buddy's&nbsp;
adoptive elf father respectively.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.040
Meanwhile, Zooey Deschanel&nbsp;
plays Ferrell's love interest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.200
Mary Steenburgen plays Buddy's stepmom,&nbsp;
and Daniel Tay is his stepbrother.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.560
The part of Buddy's biological father is played&nbsp;
by the incomparable James Caan. Unsurprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:23.680
the movie has inspired impassioned calls for&nbsp;
a sequel for years, yet it's never happened.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.880
In 2020, Caan gave an incredibly insightful&nbsp;
interview about "Elf," even explaining the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.160
reason we'll likely never see a&nbsp;
sequel. According to the actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:37.680
tension and personality clashes between the film's&nbsp;
star, Will Ferrell, and its director, Jon Favreau,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:42.240
mean that fans will probably never see the&nbsp;
beloved cast reunite on the big screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:47.680
In an impromptu interview with local radio&nbsp;
show 92.3's "Bull and Fox," Caan revealed,

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:52.560
"The director [Jon Favreau] and Will didn't get&nbsp;
along very well [...] So, Will wanted to do it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.120
he didn't want the director, and he had it in&nbsp;
his contract — it was one of those things."

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:58.560
"That's, that's ridiculous."

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:03.760
Caan only ended up appearing on the radio show&nbsp;
after his assistant, who is from Cleveland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.560
called into "Bull and Fox" to discuss the Browns'&nbsp;
most recent win. The actor eventually joined the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:12.960
conversation, delighting fans by sharing&nbsp;
that an "Elf" sequel very nearly happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.160
and that he had been ready to&nbsp;
sign on. This was, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:20.720
a major shift from his reluctance to appear&nbsp;
in the first film. Caan told the radio show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:24.800
hosts that he had initially turned down&nbsp;
the request to play Ferrell's father.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:26.960
"Alright, let's get it over with."

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:28.720
As Caan said,

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:32.320
"Will called me one night and said,&nbsp;
'Jimmy, want to do this picture with me?'"&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.320
Caan replied to Ferrell's request&nbsp;
negatively at first, saying,

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:41.040
"Listen, Will, I love you, you're a funny guy,&nbsp;
but I'm not doing a picture called 'Elf,' okay?"

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:43.680
Caan said he negotiated to change the film title&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.840
to "Elk" on the working scripts as&nbsp;
a condition of his casting, adding,

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.920
"I said, 'I'll tell you what, if you put a&nbsp;
K on the end of it — like just through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:56.480
working scripts [...] then I'll do it [...]'&nbsp;
It was great fun with Will. He's a great guy."

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:01.120
Following the incredible success of "Elf," the&nbsp;
actor shared that he was ready for a sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:05.280
despite the film's two biggest&nbsp;
creatives butting heads. Caan revealed,

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:09.600
"We were gonna do it, and I thought, 'Oh&nbsp;
my god, I finally got a franchise movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.200
I could make some money, let my kids&nbsp;
do what the hell they want to do.’"

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:18.000
However, personality clashes may not be the&nbsp;
only reason we haven't seen an "Elf 2" yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.960
Both Favreau and Ferrell keep pretty busy. The&nbsp;
director is currently attached to several Disney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:27.760
projects including Season 3 of "The Mandalorian."&nbsp;
Meanwhile, the "Saturday Night Live" alum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.640
continues his on-screen work in surprise hits&nbsp;
like "Eurovision Song Contest: The Story of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:38.320
Fire Saga," as he juggles producing obligations&nbsp;
on projects like "Succession" and "Dead to Me."

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:42.160
Sadly, it seems like the odds of an&nbsp;
"Elf 2" occurring just got even lower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:47.840
as James Caan tragically passed away on July&nbsp;
6, 2022. Caan's straight man to Ferrell's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:52.320
crazy man-turned-elf was a big reason why&nbsp;
the film was so hilarious and touching,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:57.360
and it's hard to imagine anyone else occupying&nbsp;
his role. It appears as though "Elf" is destined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:01.520
to be a one-and-done-type project. At least&nbsp;
fans can take solace in knowing all the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:06.800
puzzle pieces came together at one place in&nbsp;
time to deliver a genuine Christmas classic.

